                <pb facs="1029" n="915" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg915"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">825.00 Revolutions/28: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Culbertson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-07T02:00:00-04:00">September 7, 1931—2
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>112. General Vergara informed me personally at midnight that the aerial
                    bombardment this afternoon completely demoralized the crews, who released their
                    officers, who thereupon attempted intercession for the men. The Government
                    replied that it would accept only an unconditional surrender and in case of
                    further resistance it would proceed even more drastically. The General added
                    significantly that he would not treat with any of the officers on board. He
                    asserted with confidence that he believes that the resistance of the insurgent
                    crews is almost at an end.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Culbertson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
